Clamp the saved cursor on resize

on_resize() brings the cursor inside the new size and leaves the saved
cursor -- the one DECSC and entering the alternate screen keep, and DECRC
and leaving it put back -- where it was. Restored on a smaller screen it
lands on a row that no longer exists, and the next character reads and
writes lineinfo past its end. Found by fuzzing BeerSSH's emulator under
ASan: four steps, a save, a resize to one row, a restore and a letter.

Clamp the saved position the way the live one is clamped.

--- a/src/state.c
+++ b/src/state.c
@@ -2145,6 +2145,15 @@
   if(state->pos.col >= cols)
     state->pos.col = cols - 1;
 
+  /* And the saved cursor, which DECRC and leaving the alternate screen
+   * put back as it was: saved on a taller screen, it came back on a row
+   * this one no longer has, and the next character wrote past the end of
+   * the row information. */
+  if(state->saved.pos.row >= rows)
+    state->saved.pos.row = rows - 1;
+  if(state->saved.pos.col >= cols)
+    state->saved.pos.col = cols - 1;
+
   /* Not cancelling the pending wrap: a reflow moves the cursor every
    * time, and whether a wrap is pending was decided just above. */
   updatecursor(state, &oldpos, 0);
